WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.340
Tor Browser is more than a regular browser pointed at a proxy.

2
00:00:04.440 --> 00:00:09.880
It is designed so many users look more similar to websites, which helps reduce browser fingerprinting.

3
00:00:09.980 --> 00:00:22.780
Fingerprinting happens when a site combines details such as window size, fonts, device features, time zone behavior, supported APIs, and script results to recognize a browser without a traditional cookie.

4
00:00:22.880 --> 00:00:30.320
A privacy network can hide the original network address, but a unique browser fingerprint can still make sessions easier to connect.

5
00:00:30.600 --> 00:00:34.200
The safer defaults in Tor Browser are built around consistency.

6
00:00:34.300 --> 00:00:39.900
The browser limits or adjusts features that can make one user stand out from the crowd.

7
00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:48.960
It includes privacy settings, script restrictions at higher security levels, isolation of site data, and careful handling of identity changes.

8
00:00:49.060 --> 00:00:56.420
These design choices can feel less convenient than a fully customized browser, but convenience is not the only goal.

9
00:00:56.520 --> 00:01:00.600
Looking ordinary within the Tor Browser population is part of the protection.

10
00:01:00.880 --> 00:01:04.880
Site data isolation is a useful example of a browser-level protection.

11
00:01:04.980 --> 00:01:12.500
In an ordinary browser, cookies and storage can make it easy for one company or embedded tracker to connect activity across many visits.

12
00:01:12.600 --> 00:01:17.560
Tor Browser is designed to reduce that kind of cross-site linking during Tor sessions.

13
00:01:17.660 --> 00:01:32.780
That does not mean tracking is impossible, but it does mean the browser is working on several layers at once: network address, stored data, script behavior, fingerprintable features, and the lifetime of browsing state in practice.

14
00:01:33.060 --> 00:01:40.260
This is why installing extra extensions, changing deep settings, or maximizing the window can work against the design.

15
00:01:40.360 --> 00:01:44.840
A rare combination of add-ons and behavior may create a more distinct browser.

16
00:01:44.940 --> 00:01:58.700
The same problem can appear when a user copies habits from a normal browser into Tor Browser, such as logging into personal accounts, allowing unnecessary permissions, or downloading files and opening them in external applications.

17
00:01:58.800 --> 00:02:02.960
The network path is only one layer; browser behavior is another layer.

18
00:02:03.240 --> 00:02:08.440
Tor Browser also gives users a new circuit option and identity controls.

19
00:02:08.540 --> 00:02:15.340
A new circuit can change the relay path for a particular site, which can help with a blocked or unreliable exit path.

20
00:02:15.440 --> 00:02:21.520
A new identity is stronger because it closes tabs and clears session state before starting fresh.

21
00:02:21.620 --> 00:02:26.100
These controls are useful, but they do not rewrite what a user already revealed.

22
00:02:26.200 --> 00:02:33.480
If you log into a named account, the site can associate that session with the account regardless of the Tor circuit.

23
00:02:33.760 --> 00:02:37.200
Security levels deserve careful attention.

24
00:02:37.300 --> 00:02:40.420
The standard level keeps the broadest web compatibility.

25
00:02:40.520 --> 00:02:49.800
Safer and safest levels reduce risky browser features, especially scripts and media behaviors that have historically carried security or fingerprinting concerns.

26
00:02:49.900 --> 00:02:52.060
Higher levels can break some sites.

27
00:02:52.160 --> 00:02:54.240
That tradeoff is intentional.

28
00:02:54.340 --> 00:03:04.260
A person reading sensitive content may accept broken interactive features; a person using a site that requires scripts may need to understand what they are enabling.

29
00:03:04.540 --> 00:03:11.180
The practical habit is to treat Tor Browser as a carefully configured tool, not as a blank canvas for personalization.

30
00:03:11.280 --> 00:03:21.120
Keep it updated from trusted sources, use its built-in controls, avoid unnecessary extensions, and separate Tor activity from personal account activity when privacy matters.

31
00:03:21.220 --> 00:03:30.580
Tor Browser helps by making good choices the default, but it still depends on the person using it to avoid choices that make the session unique or self-identifying.
